## 1. Documentation in code

- Every exported function, class, hook, component, or API handler MUST have a documentation block in **<<CODE_COMMENT_LANGUAGE>>**.
- The block MUST include: a one-line summary, a parameters section, a return section, and (when relevant) an example call.
- The doc block MUST be written so that a **non-technical handover reader** can guess what the function does without reading the body.
- For TypeScript / JavaScript: use JSDoc.
- For Python: use Google-style docstrings.
- For Go: use the standard `// FunctionName ...` comment style.
- For other languages: pick the canonical convention and stick to it for the whole project.

Example (JSDoc):

```ts
/**
 * Returns the next sequential atomic-prompt ID by scanning the prompts folder.
 *
 * Scans `atomic-prompts/` for files matching `^\d{4}-`, parses the highest
 * existing number, and returns it incremented by one (zero-padded to 4 digits).
 *
 * @param promptsDir Absolute or workspace-relative path to the prompts folder.
 * @returns Zero-padded 4-digit string, e.g. "0007".
 * @example nextPromptId("atomic-prompts") // "0042"
 */
export function nextPromptId(promptsDir: string): string { ... }
```

## 2. Files & folders

- One responsibility per file. If a file mixes concerns (UI + data fetching + business rules), split it.
- File naming convention: <<FILE_NAMING>>.
- Folder naming convention: <<FOLDER_NAMING>>.
- Components: <<COMPONENT_NAMING>>.
- Hooks / composables: <<HOOK_NAMING>>.
- Tests live next to the file they test, named `<file>.test.<ext>` (or stack equivalent).

## 3. Error handling

- No empty `catch` blocks.
- Errors that originate from user input → render a user-readable message.
- Errors that originate from the system → log with context and rethrow OR surface a user-facing fallback.
- All async functions must handle their failure path explicitly.
- Never `console.log` an error in production code — use the project's logger.

## 4. Loading / empty / error states

Every screen that fetches data MUST handle these explicitly:

- **Loading** — a visible skeleton or spinner; no blank canvas.
- **Empty** — a labelled empty state with a call to action.
- **Error** — a labelled error state with a retry button and a support hint.

This rule is enforced at code-review time.

## 5. Forms

- Client-side validation runs on blur and on submit.
- Server-side validation runs on submit; client trusts only server-side outcomes for state changes.
- Every error from validation must point at the specific field.
- Submit buttons disable while a submission is pending.
- Passwords / tokens / 2FA codes use the right HTML input attributes (`autocomplete`, `inputmode`, `type`).

## 6. Imports & dependencies

- No wildcard imports unless the convention of the language demands it.
- No unused imports. Lint-enforced.
- Adding a new dependency requires a `### Security` note in `CHANGELOG.md` describing why and a quick supply-chain check (popularity, maintenance, license).

## 7. Logging & telemetry

- Use the project logger (<<LOGGER_NAME>>), never `console.*` in production code.
- Never log secrets, full tokens, full PII, or full request bodies.
- Errors logged in the backend must include: request ID (if any), user ID (if available), timestamp, and stack trace.

## 8. Database access (full rules in `DATABASE.md`)

- Use the project's data layer (<<DATA_LAYER>>). No ad-hoc queries scattered across components.
- Never compose SQL with string concatenation. Use parameter binding / the ORM.
- Every write that touches user data goes through a function that respects the auth context.

## 9. Authentication & authorisation

- Never trust client-side claims for authorisation. Always re-check on the server.
- Tokens / sessions handled by the chosen auth provider (<<AUTH_STACK>>).
- Sign-out clears local state completely (storage, in-memory caches, query caches).

## 10. Tests

- Every new public function should have at least one happy-path test and one failure-path test.
- UI components need at least an "it renders" test and an "it handles the error state" test.
- Tests must be deterministic. No network in unit tests.

## 11. Mobile & responsive

- All screens MUST be tested at <<MIN_VIEWPORT_WIDTH>>px width before merge.
- Touch targets minimum 44×44 px (or platform equivalent).
- No hover-only interactions for primary actions.

## 12. Commit & PR hygiene

- Commit message style: <<COMMIT_MESSAGE_STYLE>>.
- Branch naming: <<BRANCH_NAMING>>.
- PR descriptions must reference any related atomic-prompt file ID.
- PRs must update the relevant doc files in the same diff as the code change.

## 13. Pragmatic POC / Lovable mode

If the project is currently in `<<POC_OR_PROD>>` mode and that mode is `POC`, the following rules are relaxed:

- Tests may be skipped for throwaway prototypes (must be noted in `STATE.md`).
- Some doc rigour may be deferred to a "hardening pass" before going to production.

When `<<POC_OR_PROD>>` flips to `PROD`, all rules above are mandatory.

## 14. Pre-commit checklist

Before considering work "done":

- [ ] Code compiles / passes the linter.
- [ ] Relevant doc files updated (`STATE.md` / `CHANGELOG.md` / `CLIENT_DOCS.md` as applicable).
- [ ] Loading / empty / error states present where relevant.
- [ ] No secrets in the diff.
- [ ] No `console.*` / debug prints left in.
- [ ] Mobile view sanity-checked.
- [ ] Tests pass (or explicit POC-mode skip is justified in `STATE.md`).
